# One row per expression host, one distinct operator each so no two rows carry the same message
# (the .error matcher binds a pattern to the FIRST diagnostic containing it). Hosts, in order:
# parenthesized tail, tuple element, call argument, keyword-argument value, subscript, slice bound,
# list element, set element, dict key, dict value, comprehension element, comprehension condition,
# comprehension iterable, f-string interpolation. Every one of them reported the generic SPY0104
# before #1790's remediation except the paren tail (#1790, plan D Decision 9(c)).
'??=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @12:20
'+=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @13:15
'-=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @14:13
'*=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @15:17
'/=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @16:16
'//=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @17:16
'%=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @18:14
'**=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @19:14
'&=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @20:14
'|=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @21:19
'^=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @22:14
'<<=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @23:37
'>>=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @24:32
'@=' is an augmented assignment and cannot appear inside an expression; use ':=' for inline assignment @25:16
# The generic bracket-mismatch errors these hosts produced before the fix must be gone. The one
# residual SPY0104 is "Expected Colon, got RightBracket" at 22:36 — recovery re-reading the tail of
# the comprehension after its element was refused — which is why the count is 15, not 14.
!Expected RightParen, got
!Expected RightBracket, got
!Expected RightBrace, got
!count 15
